*(
    title: Velocity as a Function of Displacement
    description: Rewriting acceleration in terms of position, so that motion can be integrated when time is not available.
    tags: mechanics, velocity, acceleration, displacement, chain rule, calculus, extension 2
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    The usual chain runs displacement to velocity to acceleration by differentiating with respect to time. It stalls whenever the force depends on where the body is rather than on when it is there, because then acceleration is known as a function of position and time never appears in the equation.

    ===

    The chain rule supplies the way round. Velocity is a function of position, and position is a function of time, so $\dfrac{dv}{dt}$ can be written as $\dfrac{dv}{dx}$ times $\dfrac{dx}{dt}$ — and $\dfrac{dx}{dt}$ is $v$ itself.

    ===

    The product $v\dfrac{dv}{dx}$ has a second name. Differentiating $\frac{1}{2}v^2$ with respect to $x$ by the chain rule gives $v\dfrac{dv}{dx}$ exactly, so acceleration is also the derivative of half the velocity squared with respect to displacement. That is the form that integrates most cleanly.

    definition{
        $\ddot{x} = \dfrac{dv}{dt} = v\dfrac{dv}{dx} = \dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\tfrac{1}{2}v^2\right)$. The first is in time; the other two are in displacement, and they are the same expression written two ways.
    }(
        term: The three forms of acceleration
    )

    Nothing physical has changed. The three expressions are one quantity, and the choice between them is decided by which variable the problem hands over.
}[
    #### Acceleration in time

    equation{
        \ddot{x} = \frac{dv}{dt}
    }

    cue{
        #### The chain rule through $x$

        equation{
            \frac{dv}{dt} = \frac{dv}{dx}\cdot\frac{dx}{dt} = v\frac{dv}{dx}
        }
    }(
        in: 1
    )

    cue{
        #### The same product as a derivative of $\tfrac{1}{2}v^2$

        equation{
            \frac{d}{dx}\left(\tfrac{1}{2}v^2\right) = v\frac{dv}{dx} = \ddot{x}
        }
    }(
        in: 2
    )
]

scene{
    # Choosing the form

    The table sets out the choice. When the acceleration is given as a function of time, the time form integrates directly, once for $v$ and again for $x$. When it is given as a function of $x$, the $\frac{1}{2}v^2$ form turns the equation into $\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{1}{2}v^2\right) = f(x)$, integrated with respect to $x$.

    When the acceleration is a function of $v$, both displacement forms are available and the question decides. Wanting $x$ in terms of $v$ calls for $v\dfrac{dv}{dx}$, which separates as $dx = \dfrac{v\,dv}{f(v)}$. Wanting $t$ in terms of $v$ calls for $\dfrac{dv}{dt}$, which separates as $dt = \dfrac{dv}{f(v)}$.

    note{
        A constant of integration appears at each integration, and it is fixed by an initial condition — the velocity at some known position, or at $t = 0$. A problem that gives no such condition cannot be finished, and a solution that forgets to use one is not finished either.
    }
}[
    table{
        Acceleration given as a function of | Wanted                  | Form to use
        time $t$                            | $v$, then $x$            | $\ddot{x} = \dfrac{dv}{dt}$, integrated twice in $t$
        displacement $x$                    | $v$ in terms of $x$      | $\ddot{x} = \dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\tfrac{1}{2}v^2\right)$
        velocity $v$                        | $x$ in terms of $v$      | $\ddot{x} = v\dfrac{dv}{dx}$
        velocity $v$                        | $t$ in terms of $v$      | $\ddot{x} = \dfrac{dv}{dt}$
    }(
        title: Which form of acceleration, and when
        header column
    )
]

scene{
    # A worked case

    A particle moves along a line with acceleration $\ddot{x} = 2x + 1$, and its velocity is $2$ when $x = 0$. The acceleration is a function of $x$, so the $\frac{1}{2}v^2$ form is the one to use.

    equation{
        \frac{d}{dx}\left(\tfrac{1}{2}v^2\right) = 2x + 1 \implies \tfrac{1}{2}v^2 = x^2 + x + C
    }

    The initial condition fixes $C$: at $x = 0$, $\frac{1}{2}(4) = C$, so $C = 2$ and $v^2 = 2x^2 + 2x + 4$.

    ===

    At $x = 2$ this gives $v^2 = 16$, and the velocity is $4$ rather than $-4$ because the particle started moving in the positive direction and $v^2$ is never zero — the quadratic $2x^2 + 2x + 4$ has no real roots — so $v$ never changes sign. The curve on the canvas is $v$ as a function of $x$, and both points sit on it.

    note{
        Taking the square root of $v^2$ always raises the question of sign. It is settled by the physics, not the algebra: the sign of $v$ at the start persists until $v$ passes through zero, and whether it ever does is read off the expression for $v^2$.
    }
}[
    canvas.graph{
        curve(expression: sqrt(2 * x^2 + 2 * x + 4); domain: [-1, 3]; colour: blue; label: $v = \sqrt{2x^2 + 2x + 4}$)
        point(x: 0; y: 2; colour: orange; label: $(0, 2)$)

        cue{
            point(x: 2; y: 4; colour: teal; label: $(2, 4)$)
        }(
            in: 1
        )
    }(
        x axis label: $x$
        y axis label: $v$
        x domain: [-1.5, 3.5]
        y domain: [0, 6]
    )
]

scene{
    ## From velocity back to time

    Once $v$ is known as a function of $x$, the time can be recovered, because $v = \dfrac{dx}{dt}$ is a differential equation in $x$ and $t$ that separates.

    ===

    Take $\ddot{x} = 4x$ with $v = 2$ at $x = 1$, the particle moving in the positive direction. The $\frac{1}{2}v^2$ form gives $\frac{1}{2}v^2 = 2x^2 + C$, and the initial condition gives $C = 0$, so $v = 2x$ — positive, since $x$ and $v$ both start positive and $v$ vanishes only at $x = 0$, which is never reached.

    ===

    Now $\dfrac{dx}{dt} = 2x$. Separating and integrating, $\ln x = 2t + C$, and taking $x = 1$ at $t = 0$ gives $x = e^{2t}$. The motion runs away exponentially, which is what an acceleration proportional to displacement and pointing away from the origin does.

    Two standard forces are described in exactly this way, and each gets its own internote. A force pulling back towards a fixed point, growing with the distance from it, gives *Simple Harmonic Motion*; a force opposing the motion and growing with the speed gives *Resisted Motion*.

    task{
        A particle moves with $\ddot{x} = -\dfrac{8}{x^3}$ and is at rest at $x = 2$. Find its velocity when it reaches $x = 1$.
    }[
        $\frac{d}{dx}\left(\tfrac{1}{2}v^2\right) = -8x^{-3}$ gives $\tfrac{1}{2}v^2 = 4x^{-2} + C$. At rest at $x = 2$: $0 = 1 + C$, so $C = -1$ and $v^2 = \dfrac{8}{x^2} - 2$. At $x = 1$, $v^2 = 6$. The acceleration is negative throughout, so the particle moves towards the origin and $v = -\sqrt{6}$.
    ](
        title: Your turn
    )

    internote(ref: simple-harmonic-motion.chalk)

    internote(ref: resisted-motion.chalk)
}[
    #### The $\tfrac{1}{2}v^2$ form, with $v = 2$ at $x = 1$

    equation{
        \tfrac{1}{2}v^2 = 2x^2 + C \implies C = 0 \implies v = 2x
    }

    cue{
        #### Now a differential equation in $x$ and $t$

        equation{
            \frac{dx}{dt} = 2x \implies \int\frac{dx}{x} = \int 2\,dt \implies \ln x = 2t + C
        }
    }(
        in: 1
    )

    cue{
        #### With $x = 1$ at $t = 0$

        equation{
            x = e^{2t}
        }
    }(
        in: 2
    )
]
